
  Third question — and "persist" is doing a lot of work in your sentence:

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether the userId has to survive a page reload, or only has to be reachable from anywhere in the app while
  the page is alive. These need different machinery, and one of them has a security consequence.

  - [1mIn-memory shared module[0m — a session module holding the current userId; any form can read it. Survives navigation
    [3mwithin[0m a single-page app, does [1mnot[0m survive a reload or a new tab. Buys reuse with no storage and no new risk. Cheap.
  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[0m — same shared module, backed by per-tab storage. Survives reload, dies when the tab closes. Buys
    "don't lose the user on refresh" at the cost of identity sitting in storage readable by any script on the page.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — same, but persists across tabs and browser restarts until explicitly cleared. Buys the longest-lived
    "stay logged in" feel, and carries the most exposure: a stored user identity that outlives the session and is
    readable by any script, including an injected one.

  [1mMy recommendation: in-memory shared module now, with the storage read/write isolated behind it.[0m Your stated need is
  "other forms can get at it", which in-memory fully satisfies. Adding [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m later is a two-line change [3minside[0m
  the module if the module is the only thing that touches storage — so building the boundary now is what keeps the
  choice cheap, and you don't take on stored-identity exposure before you need it.

  Worth naming: whichever you pick, a [38;5;153muserId[39m in browser storage is a client-side claim, not proof of identity. It's fine
  for "which forms prefill for whom" and not fine as an authorization signal — the server has to re-derive identity
  from the session on every request that matters. That doesn't change the choice below; it changes what the value is
  allowed to be used for, and I'll write it into the spec.
[38;5;246m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
[38;5;16m[48;5;153m ☐ Persistence [39m[49m

[1m[38;5;231mHow long does the userId need to survive?[0m

[38;5;153m❯[39m [38;5;246m1.[39m [38;5;153mIn-memory module (Recommended)[39m
     [38;5;246mShared session module, reachable from any form. Lost on reload. No storage, no new exposure; storage can be added [39m
     [38;5;246minside it later.[39m
  [38;5;246m2.[39m sessionStorage (per tab)
     [38;5;246mSurvives reload, dies with the tab. Identity sits in storage readable by any script on the page.[39m
  [38;5;246m3.[39m localStorage (across restarts)
     [38;5;246mSurvives tabs and browser restarts until cleared. Longest-lived, most exposure; needs an explicit clear on logout.[39m
  [38;5;246m4. Type something.[39m
